{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at 1e9d02175efd533d4f02dce8c49a9569e7dfdef3: the five pages citing the retired shipped UI pointer are re-anchored, and one drift they were hiding is corrected.\n\nWHAT WAS BROKEN. The focus-pins merge retired the shipped UI predecessor f6c96085, and five pages cited it: docs/concepts/components.md, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/limits.md, docs/reference/gs/serve.md and docs/reference/mcp/presence.md. A retired citation is a fatal class in the citation gate, so main was red.\n\nI asked for that retirement. It was correct — step five wants the predecessor withdrawn once the merge lands — but I checked only which artifacts and acts depended on it and never which pages cited it, despite having run exactly that page-citation check before my own batch of fifteen retirements earlier the same day. I applied the discipline to retirements I performed and skipped it for one I asked someone else to perform.\n\nRE-CHECKED, NOT SWAPPED. Each page was read against what main carries at 2327e0ed.\n\nFour were accurate and needed only the citation. presence.md is worth naming because I expected it to be wrong: it claims the displayed note is chosen under locale-aware string ordering, and the focus union next to it uses a plain sort, so I suspected drift. The code selects the note with localeCompare at interaction.ts:295, and caps the focus union at eight events in three places. The page is right and my suspicion was not.\n\nlimits.md had drifted. It said the browser's event railway is a newest-80 window that says when it is truncated. The bound is real but belongs to the commit graph: internal/service/ui.go:59-61 caps the served graph at 80 commits and marks the response truncated, server_test.go:352 pins that, and WorkDrawer.tsx:260 renders \"Showing the newest 80 commits.\" The event railway is not windowed that way; it folds lanes when it runs out of room. The page now says which component has the bound and what the railway does instead. Citing the merged artifact beside the old sentence would have left a wrong claim reading as verified, which is worse than the dangling citation it replaced.\n\nNOTHING ADDED TO THE BASELINE. citation_baseline.txt is untouched. These five were a regression minutes old, not inherited debt, and baselining them would have converted a fresh break into permanently tolerated damage.\n\nGATES at this exact head: the citation gate green, the full internal/docset suite uncached green at 82s, make docs green, git diff --check clean. The diff is five documentation pages and nothing else.\n\nONE THING THE REQUEST DID NOT COVER, for whoever merges. The same retirement also staled the docs artifacts themselves: codex's 8c9b7432 rests directly on f6c96085, and my back-pressure docs candidate dbc337a0 is stale too. So both live artifacts at this path are stale, and this one deliberately rests on the request and my promise rather than on either predecessor, so that it is not stale from birth. The succession at docs still needs settling, and it needs an author or a ratifier for codex's.","body":{"commit":"1e9d02175efd533d4f02dce8c49a9569e7dfdef3","path":"docs"}}